Date: Sat, 06 Nov 93 01:04:27 GMT
From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Subject: Future Science

Phil corrects me:

> >Darwin arrived at his theory over a number of years, after
> >extensive reading of economists, as well as naturalists. Early
> >liberal economics provided the inspiration for biology.
> 
> I think you're making a slight mistake here. Darwin arrived at
> his theory after the above reading _and_ one hell of a lot of
> field work. Biology owes as much, and probably a lot more, to
> the mountains of data collected by Darwin and many others, than
> to classical liberalism.

Oops.  Thanks for the correction.  I should have said "after extensive
reading after the voyage of the Beagle".  Minimising the role of
Darwin's field work was not my intention.  I was trying to dispel the
sometimes-heard belief that Darwin was so impressed by the variety of
life he encountered that he realised how natural selection worked, there
and then, while aboard ship.  The truth of the matter (according to
Gould and others) is that he only arrived at his conclusions about
natural selection after a long and careful search of the literature,
upon his return (and then he didn't publish for decades).

