Date: Tue, 09 Nov 93 02:07:22 EST
From: wit@MIT.EDU (paul whitmore)
Subject: Physics as Therapy

>    how can i avoid being enmired in bullshit? what will keep me 
honest? 
>    what will help me cut through the jargon, the shared delusions, the 
false 
>    commitments? 
> 
> Physics.

succinct.
but it does not innoculate you from BS--many people have blown their 
minds thinking about qmechanics. 

this really raises the big question: do i choose rigor, lasting 
knowledge, structured in precise logically tight formalisms, or should i 
choose some form of inquiry that will manage to REFER to the world, as 
it is, sometime before the year 5000? 

if i want to be involved in the world, then fly away rigor. there is no 
clarity in the mire of real application. MDs are probably the paragons 
of line crossers. with statistical methods, incredible doses of hubris, 
they have in a vast number of cases produced therapies that are more 
harmful than the original disease. and they do not know when they are 
right. the most important part of their knowledge construction 
enterprise is the ego. it is not the method. 
i am not advocating homeopathy here. there are many definite advances 
due to microbiology, etc. but doctors know they are sticking their hands 
into guts, pulling out irregular shaped pieces of gristle, and just 
getting on with it.

maybe in studying probability you get to see how the mathematical world 
slips down into the actuarial one we inhabit. 

i am very keen, right now, to study statistics, develop some 
understanding of how to measure for significance, look for real 
interactions, get a little more sophisticated on what an analysis of 
variation actually entails.

the obstacle to clarity here, to quote john klein, is being forced to 
use shit for brains as my ultimate resolving lens. i guess it is a 
partial cop out to claim that i cannot *get* mathematically precise 
ideas to stay put in my brain. i do not think i have ever hacked my way 
to the end of even a 4-line proof. my brain really is mush when i need 
to generate a clear path to the end of a logical entailment. this has 
not stopped me from being increasingly interested in mathematical ideas. 

at times, softshells like me are paralysed with math-envy. Still, math 
seems to be just another kind of language, and although it is hard to 
construct contradictions by using it to express ideas, i am sure that 
most of what mathematicians say, in proofs and to one another, has as 
high a ratio of bullshit, vagueness, leaping imprecision and 
overgenerality, as one finds in most intense intellectual discussions. i 
just don't believe that using differential equations to "talk about a 
process" necessarily leads to greater insight or accuracy. most things 
human-built are ugly and quivering kludges. this must be just as likely 
in real mathematical thought as in other things human.

Cut to the quick:
Yes, it wd be great to build very rigorous descriptions, modeled on 
Gibbs discussion of TD. but that lead to millions of overly precise 
pieces of shit in ai. aristotle advised: "use the right tool for the 
job." 

i am trying to model my own education on the accumulation of knowledge 
in the physical sciences. it is disgusting to me to invest all my energy 
in an endeavor that is mud, sludge, assumptions piled on opinions 
touching on facts. it is infuriating that almost no one in 
education/psychology takes time to think through to the fundamental 
implications of an idea. without this kind of thought, researchers are 
continually re-assuming, cross-inventing, undoing one another's thought. 
(David Marr, in his personal vision of AI, talked about two forms of a 
theory: type 1 is the minimal, rigorous, result. once done, it never has 
to be redone, and later researchers can use the "result" as an atom in a 
larger theory. type 2 is the kludge, a hunk of opportunistic 
assumptions, and the only reason it's that way rather than another is 
just bc: maybe the researcher building the theory ran out of 
thought-steam, maybe someone else poked in then, but it's just a fucking 
heap of assumptions, and somehow, it works. but the slightest shift in 
the sand mountain, and the relationship between every element is 
hopelessly changed. you can take the whole hunk of junk, or leave it. 
but there's nothing modular or extendible about the mound.)
conversation, life, *interestingness*: 3 examples of things that are 
mound-like. the clue seems to be the relational dependence on context 
(itself a word that should only be klanged when a theory is about to be 
sent into the realm of the shades).
somehow, as people, we have figured out how to break through neat 
logical knots on the impossibility of fully specifying context. problems 
of reference, vicious circles, the frame problem etc. 
but what's goose and what's gander? i mean, just bc we somehow can make 
powerful presuppositions that save us from faling into the infinite 
regresses of a formalism, does that mean we should erect academic 
departments where people spend all there time practicing such a feat? 
well, who really cares what academics are licensed to do. it seems clear 
that each discipline is pretty much defined by its shared sense of what 
is solid, what can be taken for granted, and what counts as a claim. 
most disciplines look upon any dependents with snide condescension. 

this is where clarity about reductionism might actually save my soul. 
souls, no, but i also don't think that it's all just 
mathematical-physics and chaff. we're biological organisms, who got this 
far by making huge kludgey leaps. these leaps are stipulated 
equivalences that are externally arbitrary and not deducible from any 
lower laws. these let us to run like mad in the middle of the darkness.
this is bullshit. by my own lights, since it could be entirely different 
under another description.
and i keep thinking we are after the parts of experience that are not so 
dependent on the language we use to talk about them. 
Hans Moravec has been claiming something very different: every 
interpretation holds. so be it. that's phenomenology. {i always say that 
to terminate discussions which begin to spin around subjectivity, 
fixity, stuffity}


