i am not going to get a degree in a hard science. that's not where my 
talents are. though gre's are not an orb, quantitative, i was 77%ile; 
analytic, 84%.

but as far as i can tell, i am very bright. like many people, somethings 
i do very well; those i equate with intelligence. then there's other 
things, that robots and zombies do, such as calculate, remember 
accurately, etc.

but i want to get as disciplined an education as i can force my mind to 
pursue. i also want to learn the Truth,which must be expressible in a 
formally described set of statements. (?)

anyway: i do not want to get intimidated by math-brains. i have 
something to say. it wd be arrogant to ignore the very clever *results* 
of others (math-envy?)

but there is so much to do. i am frequently surprised that i know more 
about important ideas than people who wd be able to understand them more 
thoroughly than i if they studied them. it's a huge world, tons of work 
for everybody.

the tension, now i see it: between listening to the truth, and 
generating good guesses. i am usu. only interested in the *truths* that 
other people find. i like listening to even my wildest guesses. but if i 
want to learn what others have discovered, i cd literally crush my 
brain, spend all my waking hours trying to stuff it with results that 
have been established by others.

it is usu. imposs. to build on another person's opinions, bc they are so 
crudded up and inarticulately rooted in implicit assumptions. 
so the academic enterprise is to take yr guesses, and somehow, shine 
them into something that looks usable to others. if you persuade a few 
people to listen, and start building on your assumptions, you're on your 
way to being trivial. hurray!

i am not going to start thinking like a physicist. my ideas don't come 
to me in the form of hardheaded truths.  yet there's no point in being a 
muddleheaded speculator. i hate bumbling impressionistics. they compete 
for my air. 

it's still the simple question, what am i sufficiently excited about to 
commit serious brain time to trying to work out? how hard am i willing 
to think? how long do i want to look for real solid understanding? 
(obv'ly these are not the questions that grad schools want to help me 
answer. so what? that's what i want to use my time in grad school to 
sort through.)

oh: the other problem. a lot of knowledge exists, but the ways for 
teaching/learning it are very limited. Uri Wilensky told me that Tufts 
has a big computer grant to develop statistics tutorials. when he looked 
it over, the method/content seemed fundamentally flawed.  i don't know 
what made him decide that. but in physics, as another e.g., andy di 
sessa studied MIT undergrads, who'd taken at least one year of physics, 
and most of them still fucked up on any questions about physics that 
required extending the knowledge outside the narrow test probe. 

and i think, i want, not to learn the test performance stuff, but how to 
pursue problems that come up in physics and math. 

this raises to the sludge problem. one physics professor claimed that 
understanding physics meant being able to solve the problems, period. 
strong identity. no such thing as understanding w/o being able to work 
the problems out. but the students at MIT who came up with ridiculous 
predictions for where a ball would come out of a circular rolling tube 
cd all solve the problems. and they had internalized the vocabulary. so 
they created bullshit predictions that defied understanding.

i am waiting for math and physics to be approachable from more than a 
mere calculational point of view. but if i do not have the brain that 
can do the calculations, why assume i can do any higher order reasoning?


